/-!
# Canonical normal Stein model over a Noetherian base

Source: Stacks Project, More on Morphisms, Tag 03H0, properties (1)--(5) and proof;
Morphisms of Schemes, Tag 035L, normal integral source case
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-normal-stein-model).
-/

@[expose] public section

set_option autoImplicit false

universe u

open CategoryTheory

namespace AlgebraicGeometry

/-- A proper dominant map of integral Noetherian schemes with normal source has its
canonical normal integral Stein model, finite over the base.

Source: Stacks Project, Tags 03H0 and 035L
(blueprint: lem-sfb-noetherian-normal-stein-model).

`IsNoetherian` means a finite affine cover by Noetherian rings, by
`isNoetherian_iff_of_finite_affine_openCover`. For an affine open `V`, finiteness of
`q` makes `q ⁻¹ᵁ V` affine. The isomorphism `g.app (q ⁻¹ᵁ V)` identifies its ring of
sections with `R_V = Γ(T, f ⁻¹ᵁ V)`, using `g ≫ q = f` to identify inverse images.
Consequently this chart is `Spec R_V`. Functoriality of section pullback identifies
`q` on this chart with `Spec (f.app V)`. Under the same section identification,
the section pullback of the restriction of `g` is the identity of `R_V`; the
Spec/global-sections adjunction therefore identifies it with the evaluation map.
Thus these are the canonical charts and maps of the relative spectrum, rather
than merely an unspecified factorization. `(f.app V).hom.Finite` says explicitly
that `R_V` is a finite module over `Γ(B, V)` via the prescribed pullback.

Proof plan, following the six steps of the node:
1. Properness gives quasi-compactness and quasi-separatedness (pqf-lem-proper-basic).
   Form the relative normalization (lem-weil-pbc-relative-normalization), with
   dominant first map, integral intermediate scheme and integral second map. The theorem
   lem-weil-pbc-normalization-normal-stalks gives normal stalks, since the source is normal.
2. The structure sheaf is quasi-coherent and generated by one locally, hence coherent
   on the locally Noetherian source (pqf-def-coherent, pqf-lem-coherent-ft-locnoeth).
   Proper coherent pushforward in degree zero (lem-pc-coherent-hi) and
   lem-weil-pbc-coherent-affine-sections-finite make every `R_V` finite over `Γ(B,V)`.
3. Restrict to each affine base open; properness survives this base change
   (lem-weil-pbc-proper-fp-permanence). The affine Stein theorem
   (lem-weil-pbc-stein-factorization-affine) makes every element of `R_V` integral
   over `Γ(B,V)` and makes evaluation proper with geometrically connected fibres.
   Thus the integral-closure chart of relative normalization is all of `R_V`.
   The affine adjunction (lem-a1-locally-ringed-space-affine-adjunction) identifies
   its first map with evaluation, since its section pullback is the identity.
4. These chart identifications glue: the integral-closure algebra is `f_* O_T`.
   Integralness and the finite chart rings make `q` finite
   (def-weil-finite-integral-morphism). Density of `f(T)` implies dominance of `q`;
   dominance of `g` is already supplied by normalization.
5. Properness of `g` is local on its target: the affine Stein restrictions give its
   separatedness, local finite type and quasi-compactness, and
   lem-weil-pbc-universally-closed-zariski-local gives universal closedness.
6. Every geometric fibre of `g` is a geometric fibre of one of these restrictions.
   The affine Stein theorem therefore supplies geometric connectedness globally. -/
theorem exists_noetherian_normal_stein_model
    {T B : Scheme.{u}} [IsIntegral T] [IsIntegral B]
    [IsNoetherian T] [IsNoetherian B]
    (f : T ⟶ B) [IsProper f] [IsDominant f]
    (hT : ∀ x : T, IsDomain (T.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
      IsIntegrallyClosed (T.presheaf.stalk x)) :
    ∃ (S : Scheme.{u}) (g : T ⟶ S) (q : S ⟶ B),
      IsIntegral S ∧ IsDominant g ∧ IsDominant q ∧ g ≫ q = f ∧
      (∀ x : S, IsDomain (S.presheaf.stalk x) ∧
        IsIntegrallyClosed (S.presheaf.stalk x)) ∧
      IsFinite q ∧ IsProper g ∧ GeometricallyConnected g ∧
      (∀ V : B.Opens, IsAffineOpen V →
        (f.app V).hom.Finite ∧ IsIso (g.app (q ⁻¹ᵁ V))) := by
  sorry

end AlgebraicGeometry
